.TH PLUGIN\-HOSTD\-PROBE 1 "October 2026" "plugin-hostd 0.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plugin\-hostd\-probe \- run a plugin prober under limits and write what happened
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plugin\-hostd\-probe run
.RI [ options ]
.I plugin.clap
.RI [ "clap id" ]
.br
.B plugin\-hostd\-probe run
.RI [ options ]
.BI lv2: uri
.br
.B plugin\-hostd\-probe describe
.RI [ options ]
.BI clap: path # id
|
.BI lv2: uri
.br
.B plugin\-hostd\-probe panel
.RI [ options ]
.BI clap: path # id
|
.BI lv2: uri
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plugin\-hostd\-probe
starts a program that loads a plugin (clap\-qualify for a CLAP, plugin\-qualify's lv2_measure for an LV2 named
.BI lv2: uri ,
the pin pass and then the measurement at the highest rate; both run under plugin\-qualify's interposer, rt_interpose.so beside
the child) in its own process group, with the portable
limits applied before the exec, and writes the facts of the run as a JSON document: the steps the child reached, how
each ended and the limits that were applied. It gives no verdict. The runner never loads plugin code itself. On the
deadline, on a cancel or when the child is done it kills the whole process tree, including a process the plugin
started and orphaned.
.PP
A bare
.B \-\-child
is found as execvp finds it; name the child by path when PATH is unset or holds a directory of its name.
The measured child gets the interposer and nothing else: an LD_PRELOAD the runner was started with is dropped for the
measurement, named in run.tools.droppedPreload and in the rt\-safety step, and put back in the runner's environment after
the child.
.PP
The measured child runs as the host runs the plugin: SCHED_FIFO at the highest priority the runner may give it, and
with its memory locked. The runner chooses the priority before any child: all of SCHED_FIFO with CAP_SYS_NICE, else
the RLIMIT_RTPRIO hard limit it was started with, else none (rtkit is not asked). The measured child takes it last
before its exec, so every thread the prober starts inherits it; the runner and the pin pass of an LV2 plugin stay
SCHED_OTHER. The sandbox bounds instead of forbidding: RLIMIT_RTPRIO is the granted priority (0 without one) and
RLIMIT_RTTIME the wall budget, past which the kernel kills a real\-time thread that never blocks. The memory is locked by
the lock preload,
.IR <libexecdir>/plugin\-hostd/probe\-lock.so ,
preloaded beside the interposer: mlockall of every current and future page, within the RLIMIT_MEMLOCK hard limit,
and only when that covers the memory limit. The document says what the kernel reported for the
child after the exec in
.B run.realtime
(class, priority, the priority requested, who granted it, and why none), never what was asked for, and whether the
lock held in
.BR run.memoryLock .
A measurement without real time is still a valid one, and says why. A service that runs the probe grants it with
.B LimitRTPRIO=
and
.B LimitMEMLOCK=
(or
.BR AmbientCapabilities=CAP_SYS_NICE ),
and without
.BR RestrictRealtime= .
.PP
The document follows
.IR plugin\-hostd/probe\-output.schema.json .
Nothing a plugin prints can enter it.
.SH DESCRIBE
.B describe
writes what a plugin states about itself as a scanned descriptor (omx\-contract's descriptors), on standard output.
It runs omx\-clap\-scan \-\-fields on the plugin, under the same limits and in a process group of its own, and reads
each field of the dump through the format's table, which plugin\-hostd\-devel installs in omx\-contract's contract
directory (3/plugin\-hostd/formats/clap.json and lv2.json): each field becomes a descriptor word, goes verbatim into
the descriptor's extensions, or is dropped by the ruling the table names. A field the table has no row for is refused.
Its options:
.TP
.BI \-\-table " file"
The format's table, in place of the installed one.
.TP
.BI \-\-fields\-from " file"
A recorded dump, in place of running omx\-clap\-scan.
.TP
.BI \-\-child " program"
The program that writes the dump. Default omx\-clap\-scan.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file" "\fR, \fB\-\-wall\-s " n
The child's standard error, and its wall clock in seconds (default 60).
.PP
It exits 0 with a descriptor, 2 with none (usage, a table it cannot read, a dump it refuses, named on standard error),
and 3 when the child made no dump.
.SH PANEL
.B panel
writes the panel a CLAP plugin publishes (org.openmixer.panel\-description/1) on standard output, as the document
.B {"plugin": {"format", "id"}, "panel": ...}
of plugin\-hostd's docs/panel.md. It runs omx\-clap\-scan \-\-json on the plugin's file, under the same limits, and
copies the
.B panel
object the scan prints for the plugin's id byte for byte: null for a plugin that serves no description, a table the
scan refused for its count as the scan wrote it. An LV2 plugin's panel is null and nothing is run. Its options are
.BR \-\-child ,
.B \-\-log
and
.B \-\-wall\-s
as for
.BR describe ,
and
.BI \-\-json\-from " file" \fR,
a recorded scan in place of running omx\-clap\-scan. It exits 0 with the document; 2 with none (usage, a scan that is
no JSON, lists no such plugin or states no panel for it, as a scanner before omx\-clap\-scan 0.20.0 does), named on
standard error; and 3 when the child made no scan.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-child " program"
The program that loads the plugin. Default clap\-qualify, and /usr/libexec/plugin\-qualify/lv2_measure for an LV2.
.TP
.BI \-\-rates " R,R,..."
Sample rates to run at. Default: omx\-contract's ORACLE_FLOOR_RATES, the rates every kernel oracle runs at, as the
runner's usage prints them.
.TP
.BI \-\-quantum " n"
Block size in frames. Default 512.
.TP
.BI \-\-blocks " n"
Blocks to process per rate.
.TP
.BI \-\-offer " EXT,..."
Host extensions to offer on top of the child's own.
.TP
.BI \-\-probe\-notes " n"
The notes a CLAP instrument that declares no voice capacity is timed holding. Passed to clap\-qualify; without it such
an instrument has no cost.
.TP
.BI \-\-hold " n"
The notes a CLAP instrument is timed holding, whatever voice capacity it declares. Passed to clap\-qualify.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Where to write the document, atomically. Default standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file"
Where to write the child's standard error. Default discarded.
.TP
.BI \-\-reason " why"
One of add, rescan, binary\-changed, on\-request, auto\-measure, reference, ci.
.TP
.BI \-\-wall\-s " n" "\fR, \fB\-\-cpu\-s " n "\fR, \fB\-\-data\-bytes " n "\fR, \fB\-\-files " n "\fR, \fB\-\-file\-bytes " n "\fR, \fB\-\-grace\-ms " n
The limits: wall clock and CPU seconds, data segment size, open files, file size, and the grace between the
terminate and the kill.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpus " list"
Pin the child to these cpus, for example "0\-3,8".
.TP
.BI \-\-require " limit"
Refuse to run unless the limit is applied. May be repeated. A limit that is asked for and not applied by this
version is listed in the document, never skipped in silence.
.TP
.BI \-\-lock\-preload " file"
The library that locks the measured child's memory. Default <libexecdir>/plugin\-hostd/probe\-lock.so, as the build was given PREFIX (/usr/libexec in the packages); the usage names it.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-limits
Print the limits a run would apply on this machine, one per line (name, mechanism, value), the ones not applied, the
real\-time grant and whether the memory would be locked, and exit; no plugin is named and no child is started.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A document was written, the run is complete and every step ended ok or was not attempted.
.TP
.B 1
A document was written and some step ended otherwise.
.TP
.B 2
No document: usage, a required limit unavailable, or the output not writable.
.TP
.B 3
No such plugin.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR plugin\-hostd (1)
